---
import AnswerPage from "../components/AnswerPage.astro";
import BenchmarkComparison from "../components/BenchmarkComparison.astro";
import { benchmarks, facts } from "../data/site";
import { pageMeta } from "../data/seo";
const updated = new Date(benchmarks.generatedAt).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { dateStyle: "long", timeZone: "UTC" });
---

<AnswerPage title="Downright Markdown rendering benchmarks" trail="Benchmarks" updated={updated} modifiedDate={benchmarks.generatedAt.slice(0, 10)} description={pageMeta.benchmarks.description} markdownPath={pageMeta.benchmarks.markdownPath ?? "/benchmarks.md"}>
  <p>Downright 1.0.18 spends less time parsing, diffing, and updating your document. These benchmarks compare the same workloads on the same Mac.</p>

  <h2>Measured improvements</h2>
  <BenchmarkComparison reference />

  <h2 id="methodology">Test conditions and limits</h2>
  <p>{benchmarks.pipeline.machine}, measured {benchmarks.pipeline.date}. {benchmarks.pipeline.qualification} The corpus is {benchmarks.pipeline.corpus}.</p>
  <p>The measured code is included in <a href={`${facts.repository}/releases/tag/auto-${benchmarks.sourceCommit}`}>Downright 1.0.18 (build 356)</a>. <a href={`${facts.repository}/blob/${benchmarks.sourceCommit}/${benchmarks.pipeline.evidencePath}`}>Raw runs and source identity</a> are available alongside the performance record.</p>
  <p>These timings cover parsing, diffing, storage, and decoration. They do not measure first-pixel launch time, visible scrolling, or complete window transitions.</p>

  <h2>Parsing, search, and memory</h2>
  <BenchmarkComparison reference earlier />
  <ul>
    {benchmarks.optimizations.rows.map((row) => <li><strong>{row.measurement}:</strong> {row.workload}. {row.statistic}.</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>The Unicode result is a p95 latency. The search result is a single measured invocation, so it does not establish a latency percentile. The memory result is an observed range across two fresh processes, measured with the kernel's lifetime maximum physical-footprint counter.</p>
  <p>{benchmarks.optimizations.memoryLimit} A later 126.2 MB observation measured opening the stress document only; it does not replace the interactive-session measurements above. Physical footprint and live heap allocations measure different things.</p>
  <p>The memory workload uses a document with 57,043 lines and 16,298 tasks. Each run opens a fresh isolated native host, enters Source, inserts seven characters, saves, undoes and saves again, scrolls down and up, and performs three further Source/Document cycles. The document's original bytes were restored exactly.</p>

  <h2>Historical pipeline baseline</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corpus:</strong> {benchmarks.corpus}</li>
    <li><strong>Date:</strong> {benchmarks.date}</li>
    <li><strong>Qualification:</strong> {benchmarks.qualification}</li>
    <li><strong>Not measured:</strong> {benchmarks.limitations.join(", ")}</li>
  </ul>

  <p>{benchmarks.corpusCorrection} The existing timings are preserved; the workload correction prevents comparing that row with a genuine 100 KB test.</p>
  <div class="answer-table-wrap">
    <table class="answer-table">
      <caption>Historical native app baseline · {benchmarks.date}; not a cross-machine guarantee.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measurement</th><th scope="col">p50</th><th scope="col">p95</th><th scope="col">Target</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {benchmarks.rows.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.measurement}</th><td>{row.p50}</td><td>{row.p95}</td><td>{row.target}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to use the numbers</h2>
  <p>Use the benchmark to detect regressions in the same corpus and build path. Do not treat it as a promise about every Mac, document, display refresh rate, or window size. The app's release checks keep the methodology and limitations visible.</p>
</AnswerPage>
